WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.150
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.860
With this lecture,
I'd like to introduce

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.239
the major players of this
class, the particles,

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:20.700
fundamental particles, but also
some of the compound particles,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.620
which play a role in
the discussions we'll

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.730
have over the next weeks.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:33.280
For centuries, people
believed that atoms

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.840
are the most fundamental
constituents of matter.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.630
The name atom comes
from the Greek atomic,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.990
which means not divisible.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:49.570
But as you know today, electrons
and nuclei build an atom.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.250
But even those nuclei are
not fundamental particles.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.410
As you see nicely
here in this picture,

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:03.420
the nucleus can be built out
of many neutrons and protons,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.640
and even those
protons and neutrons

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.620
are not fundamental particles.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.070
A proton, for example,
as depicted here,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.640
has three components
and three constituents,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.070
two up quarks and
one down quark.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.976
A neutron, then, is built out
of one down quark and two up

00:01:20.976 --> 00:01:21.650
quarks.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:25.590
It's kind of important to
understand and appreciate

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.650
the size of those particles,
specifically the difference

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:32.700
in size.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.420
Comparing here an atom, a
typical atom, of the size of 10

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.120
to the minus 10 meters,
and that compares

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.210
to a nucleon, which can be a
few 10 to the minus 15 meters.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.820
When we talk about a
proton, we typically

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.390
like to use units
of femtometers,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.630
which is 10 times 10
to the minus 15 meters.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:01.000
The tremendous size different
or the expansive finding

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.550
of the famous gold
foil experiment, which

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.160
found that an atom
basically is made out

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.669
of a nothingness, empty
space, and a very dense

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:12.560
charged core, the nucleus.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.420
So you see this very
much in this picture

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.860
and by comparing those
order of magnitudes.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.550
Not shown in this picture
here is a particle which

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.390
doesn't really like to interact
with anybody else or which

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.720
the forces it's interacting with
are so weak that it cannot be

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.630
found, and that is a neutrino.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.790
A neutrino is not that
different from an electron

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.380
or from a quark.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:40.330
It's just that the
interactions it participates in

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.970
are only the big force
as we understand today.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.370
Just to be clear, when I talk
about a fundamental particle,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.140
I talk about a particle
which has no size,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.920
it's infinitely small.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.890
It has no substructure,
meaning it cannot be broken up

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.240
into constituents and
can also not be excited.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.470
Having said that, this is our
current understanding of nature

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.390
and of those particles.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.180
Experimentally, we can
only probe those particles

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.780
to a certain scale
or size, and we'll

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.360
talk later about how
precisely we actually

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.700
do know that a
quark is fundamental

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.822
or an electron is fundamental.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.680
In this discussion here
and most of the lecture,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.170
I talk about the standard
model in particle physics.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.330
It is a fact that
our measurements

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.420
and our experimental
findings are

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:56.110
in fantastic agreement with
this very predictive theory.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.160
The only experimental
deviation from this

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.260
is the fact that we measure
the mass of neutrinos

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.630
to be non-zero.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.530
As a consequence, you could say
the standard model is broken

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.870
or we found physics
beyond the standard model,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.090
but it is actually
rather straightforward

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.850
to extend the standard model
to accommodate neutrino masses.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.630
So we can just forget
about this small fact

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.060
and assume that the standard
model describes nature

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.530
as we know it.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:28.660
In the last week of this class,
we'll talk about motivation,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.240
why we thinks that the
standard model, in fact,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.860
is not complete, and one of the
big drivers here is the fact

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:41.510
that we cannot describe
all observations in nature,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.740
specifically the
observation of dark matter,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.143
with the standard model
in particle physics.

00:04:47.143 --> 00:04:48.310
But that's for a later date.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.740
Looking into some more
detail, the standard model

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.660
has sets of particles,
some particles which

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.660
carry forces and some
are metaparticles.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.760
The ones who carry forces
are all spin-one particles,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.580
and their bosons.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.380
We have, in the standard model,
describe three interactions.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.270
The electromagnetic
interactions,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:24.330
which are known from light,
electromagnetic phenomena,

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.620
chemistry.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.890
The atom's behavior
molecule is determined

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.997
by electromagnetic interactions.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:33.580
And then there's a
strong interaction.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.610
The name already tells
you that it's strong.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.250
It's very strong.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.840
The first carrier
here is the gluon.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.810
The gluons, there's
eight, and then

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:49.450
differentiated by
the so-called color,

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.700
which is an interesting effect.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.730
And then, there is the
weak interaction carried

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.430
by the W boson and the Z boson.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.250
They are different in their own
right because they carry mass.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.630
They are massive particles.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:07.220
And they're actually quite
heavy, about 80 to 100 times as

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.390
heavy as a proton.

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.690
The weak interaction is
responsible for neutron decays,

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:17.390
also responsible for
the burning of the sun.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:20.900
And in our nuclear physics part,
we talk in detail about what

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.580
that all means.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.290
Gravitational effects
are not considered

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:25.250
in the standard model.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.980
They are very,
very weak compared

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:31.890
to the strength of the other
forces we'll discuss here.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.740
But it's technically
very difficult

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.400
to actually accommodate
gravity as part

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.920
of a quantum [INAUDIBLE] theory.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.880
And therefore, we will
simply ignore this fact.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.082
And this is yet
another reason why

00:06:46.082 --> 00:06:47.540
you can consider
the standard model

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:53.700
to be incomplete as a model
or theory describing nature.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.190
The matter particles
themselves are all fermions.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.480
They have spin half.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.520
And they come in three
different generations.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.970
The only difference between
one generation to the next

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:07.870
is the fact that those
particles have different mass.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.360
In other words, their
coupling to the Higgs field

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:11.350
is different.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.970
And that's the only difference
between those particles.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.050
There's consequences
out of this, for example

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.250
heavier particles indicating
to lighter particles.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.040
We differentiate between
the quarks and leptons.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.370
Quarks partake in the
strong interactions,

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.940
while electrons are neutral
in the strong interaction.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:32.800
And then we have seen
neutrinos already

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.620
and, electron-type particles.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.590
Electron-type particles,
charged leptons,

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.060
they have electric charge.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.930
So they couple to photons
by neutrinos bond.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.670
I'll show you here again one
of those striking differences.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:54.430
An electron is 9 times 10 to
the minus 31 kilograms heavy.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:57.800
We like to talk about
units in a different class.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.570
But that's 511 keV.

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:05.770
While the neuron is about 200
times heavier, and even heavier

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:06.600
the tau lepton.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.920
And as I said
before, that allows

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.050
that tau lepton [INAUDIBLE]
neurons to decay

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.360
into lighter particles.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.680
We'll talk about this and how
we can use those decays in order

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.930
to learn about the
standard model.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.300
And then there is
the Higgs boson.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.380
And again, the Higgs
boson, discovered in 2012,

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:30.590
plays a very special role
in the standard model.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:34.429
We talk about the
Lagrange is a theory which

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.294
describes the standard model,
or which is the basic--

00:08:37.294 --> 00:08:39.000
is a theory itself.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.664
And later on, in this theory
we introduce a potential which

00:08:42.664 --> 00:08:46.700
is shown here-- it's
this Mexican hat--

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.530
if you want-- potential.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:51.860
And what you see here
is that in this picture,

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.290
that the lowest energy
state of this potential

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.950
breaks a symmetry.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.910
So it's away from the 0 point.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.670
And that symmetry
breaking then gives mass

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.000
to the W and the Z boson,
which I was describing before.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.810
And the coupling of meta
particles to the Higgs field

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.730
gives mass at this point.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:12.380
All this on a later date.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.960
I thought here, I'd also
give you how CERN actually

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:18.500
depicts the Higgs boson.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.900
July 4th is a special
day in the US,

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.930
but it's also a
special day at CERN,

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.930
because the Higgs discovery
has been announced on this day.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.650
And at CERN, typically, the
menu is enriched on that day

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:35.240
by the Higgs boson itself
in the form of pizza, which

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.640
takes the shape of
event displays--

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.250
displays of
proton-proton collisions

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:43.400
into the Higgs boson
and then further decays.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.250
We'll look at some of those
event displays also later.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.810
Well, that completes the
elementary-- the fundamental

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:54.760
particles of the standard
model, particles which describe

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.750
almost everything around us.

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.350
So we have seen the charged
and neutral leptons.

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:00.550
We have seen the quarks.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:04.000
We have seen the force carriers,
with the W/Z bosons, the photon

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.940
and the gluon, and
the Higgs boson, which

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.493
is kind of the very special
particle holding this

00:10:10.493 --> 00:10:11.035
all together.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.400
An interesting point
here is, again,

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:20.300
looking a little bit at history
and when those particles have

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:24.200
been discovered, and also
when those particles have

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:29.000
been explained--

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.170
and I don't want to
read this all to you.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:34.160
You see that the earliest
discovery was a discovery

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.210
by J.J. Thomson of the
electron, and the latest,

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:41.250
the completion of the particles
in the standard model--

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.910
the Higgs boson-- in 2012.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:46.820
Interesting for the
Higgs boson, the time

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:50.080
between the
theoretical discovery

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.920
by Peter Higgs and
friends was about 50 years

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.720
before the
experimental discovery.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.400
But then there is also
composite particles.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.072
The things around you are
all composite particles.

00:11:04.072 --> 00:11:08.770
Here, we can differentiate
between mesons and baryons.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:10.690
Mesons are particles
which are made out

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.560
of quarks and antiquark pairs
[? for the ?] bound states.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.430
And they are bosonic, because
you add 2 1/2 particles

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:19.480
together.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.920
One example is a pion,
which is made out

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.490
of up quarks and
down quarks that can

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:27.220
be charged in neutral pions.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.760
And the zoo of particle
increases quite quickly

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.450
if you then consider that it's
not just up and down quarks

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:35.630
making those particles.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.340
But you could add strangeness to
that, meaning a strange quark.

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:45.490
And so you see here in this
picture, the mesons, the pions,

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:48.730
etas, but also then neutrons
and charged particles

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.310
with different charges,
and then kaons,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:53.080
which are particles
which have one

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.598
s quark in addition to an
up quark and down quark.

00:11:56.598 --> 00:11:57.640
And then there's baryons.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.440
They are made out
of three quarks.

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.900
We have already seen the
protons and neutrons.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:06.080
But also here, you can see
a different configuration.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:09.280
We'll introduce the
concept of isospin.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.200
You can see here the
proton and the neutron,

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.690
and then strangeness being
added with one or two

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:14.950
components of strangeness.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:17.920
And then there is this
isospin component as well.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.220
This situation becomes
complex very, very quickly.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.970
But we'll look at
this in more detail.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:27.170
And then again,
putting those bound

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.550
states together-- bound
states of protons and neutrons

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.470
through the strong
force gives us

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:36.310
a rich table of nuclei
on isotope tape.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:40.300
Here, you can describe nuclei
by the number of protons, which

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:43.010
is typically called Z, and
the number of neutrons,

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.530
which is typically
called N. The sum,

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.535
N plus Z is the atomic mass.

00:12:48.535 --> 00:12:52.720
Now, each proton and neutron
are about 1 geV heavy.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.730
And then the atomic mass
is simply the sum of them.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.180
So you already know
explicitly how heavy

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.975
your isotope might be.

00:13:00.975 --> 00:13:03.100
We'll talk about the fact
that those masses are not

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.830
quite the sum of
the masses later,

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:09.250
because there is some
binding energy involved.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:12.790
And then isotopes can
be stable or unstable.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:15.190
They can decay in
various processes.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.170
You can combine them.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:19.760
It's very interesting to
understand how they're actually

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.930
being created in our solar
system or the universe

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:24.770
in general.

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.650
So with this, I would
like to conclude

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:28.410
this part of the introduction.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:31.810
So we have seen
the major players

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.830
of this course with the
fundamental particles,

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:40.520
but also the compound particles,
meson, baryons, and nuclei.

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.710
And there's a few more
points in the introduction

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.290
before we then dive
into a little bit more

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:48.220
of the theoretical discussion.

